window.RG = window.RG || {};
var RG = window.RG;

RG.lifeIntro = [
  "Many of us were handed a picture of Christian love that quietly asked us to disappear. Good Christians, we learned, always say yes, never get angry, never confront, never leave, never protect themselves, and never think about their own needs. If it hurt, that was the cross. If we felt used, that was pride. If we said no, that was selfishness. Many sincere believers carried this picture for years, wondering why their faith left them exhausted, anxious and easy to exploit, and why the people around them who took the most seemed to be praised the least for changing.",
  "This section looks at that picture honestly. It asks where it came from, what Scripture actually says, and what kind of love Jesus actually lived. The relational gospel begins with a simple claim: <strong>God is love</strong> (1 John 4:8), and Jesus is what that love looks like in a human life. So the measure of love is not what keeps everyone else comfortable. The measure is Christ. And in Christ we see that the Father, Son and Spirit live in a communion of love where each is fully personal and fully honoured. We were made in that image, and so we were made for love that gives freely without being erased.",
  "When we look at Jesus we see someone who gave himself completely, and also someone who rested, withdrew, said no, named lies, turned tables, refused to be pushed off a cliff, and asked the soldier who struck him, “why do you beat me?” (John 18:23). His self-giving was free, not extracted. “No one takes it away from me, but I lay it down by myself” (John 10:18). That is love with a spine. In this section you will find the case step by step, common myths and where they came from, the difference between selfishness and healthy self-care, practical steps for when you are mistreated, real-life scenarios to sort, hard questions with careful answers, and sources you can check for yourself.",
  "We will also be fair. Some Christians worry that talk of self-worth and boundaries is just modern self-help dressed in church clothes. That concern deserves a hearing, and there is a real selfishness that Scripture warns against (Phil 2:21; 2 Tim 3:2). It is also true that evangelicals themselves wrote some of the most widely read Christian books <em>for</em> healthy boundaries, such as Henry Cloud and John Townsend’s <em>Boundaries</em> (1992). The goal is not to swing from self-erasure to self-worship, but to find the shape of real love.",
  "A word about safety. If you are in danger right now, or someone is hurting you or a child, please put this page down and get help: call your local emergency number, contact an abuse or crisis hotline, and reach out to a trusted person. Nothing in Christian faith asks you to stay where you are being harmed. Forgiveness never requires you to remain in danger, and love never requires you to trust someone who is not safe."
];

RG.lifeCase = [
  {
    id: "what-we-were-taught",
    kicker: "Step 1 · The inherited picture",
    title: "What many of us were taught",
    body: [
      "Many believers grew up with a set of unspoken rules. Saying no is unloving. Confronting someone is unkind. Setting a limit is a lack of faith. Feeling angry is sin. Needing rest is laziness. Thinking about your own wellbeing is selfish. These rules were rarely written down, but they were taught through sermons, songs, Sunday school, and the way people were praised or shamed. For many, the hardest part was that these rules came wrapped in genuine faith and real love for Jesus. Questioning them felt like questioning him.",
      "One popular children’s acronym summed it up: <strong>JOY</strong> means <em>Jesus first, Others second, You last</em>. The intention was good: to teach generosity and to put God at the centre. But many children absorbed something else, that they themselves did not really count, and that their needs, feelings and safety came last, always, no matter what. Nobody seems to know exactly who first coined the acronym; it spread through twentieth-century Sunday schools and youth ministries. Underneath it was a real truth: love does put others first, and Jesus did serve. The problem was not generosity, but the word “last”, heard as “not at all”.",
      "Alongside it came phrases that sound biblical but were often bent. “Die to self” came to mean “have no self”. “Turn the other cheek” came to mean “let them hit you again”. “Forgive and forget” came to mean “act as if it never happened, and trust them again”. “Submit” came to mean “obey whoever has power over you, whatever they do”. “Bear all things” came to mean “put up with anything”. Each of these phrases has a real biblical root. That is what made them so powerful, and why it matters to read those roots carefully in context rather than throw them away.",
      "Some heard it most loudly about themselves. Phrases like “you are nothing”, “you are a worm”, or “there is nothing good in you” were meant to magnify grace, but in many hearts they became a quiet verdict: you are worthless, and the right response to mistreatment is to accept it because you deserve worse. That verdict is not the gospel. The good news Jesus announced was good news to the poor, release to captives, sight to the blind, and freedom for the oppressed (Luke 4:18). A message that leaves the oppressed more trapped, and the bruised more crushed, has lost something essential along the way (Isa 42:3). Many people who absorbed this message never heard anyone say it outright. They simply learned, from tone and emphasis, that the safest thing was to think as little of themselves as possible, and that anyone who seemed confident or self-respecting was probably proud.",
      "This step is not about blaming anyone. Many of those who taught these things loved us and were passing on what they had received. But it is important to name what we were taught, because we cannot examine a belief we have never put into words. As you read, you might notice which of these phrases you heard most often, and how they made you feel. Some people feel relief when they see them named. Others feel anxious, as if questioning them is itself disobedient. Both reactions are understandable. The invitation here is simply to bring these beliefs into the light of Christ and see whether they look like him (2 Cor 4:6)."
    ],
    refs: ["Matt 16:24", "Matt 5:39", "1 Cor 13:7", "Eph 5:21", "Ps 22:6"]
  },
  {
    id: "where-it-came-from",
    kicker: "Step 2 · Roots",
    title: "Where it came from",
    body: [
      "These ideas did not drop from the sky. They grew from several strands, some of them good things pushed out of balance. <strong>Pietism</strong> (from the late 1600s) rightly called people to heartfelt faith and self-examination, but in some forms intense inward scrutiny became a habit of constant self-suspicion. <strong>Revivalism</strong> (from the 1700s and 1800s) rightly preached repentance, but some preaching relied on making people feel as low as possible so that they would turn to God. Revival movements did enormous good, and many people found real freedom through them. But in some settings the emotional pattern became: the lower you feel, the closer you are to God. That pattern can be devastating for people who already feel worthless, or who are being told by an abuser that they are nothing. It is worth adding that many of these movements also produced great works of mercy, from orphanages to the campaign against slavery. Their weaknesses sit alongside real strengths.",
      "Hymns carried these themes into the heart. Isaac Watts wrote in 1707, “Would he devote that sacred head / For such a worm as I?” Later hymnals often changed the line to “for sinners such as I”. Watts was echoing Psalm 22:6, a lament that Jesus himself prays on the cross. But a psalm of suffering can become a definition of identity, and critics later called this outlook “worm theology”. Watts himself was a gifted and compassionate pastor, and many of his hymns celebrate God’s love warmly; the point is not to condemn him, but to notice how one image could shape a whole culture’s self-understanding.",
      "Some <strong>legal and penal frameworks</strong> of salvation described human beings mainly as guilty defendants before a judge. Those who hold them see this as faithful to texts like Romans 3. The relational gospel’s concern is not that sin is real (it is), but that when guilt becomes the deepest thing said about a person, people learn to see themselves first as offenders, and so to accept harm as what they deserve. Evangelical and Reformed theologians would answer, fairly, that penal substitution also speaks of God’s great love (Rom 5:8), and that guilt is meant to lead to grace, not despair. The concern here is with how the framework was sometimes preached and heard.",
      "<strong>Purity and shame cultures</strong> taught that a good person never makes trouble, never embarrasses the community, and keeps up appearances. And some churches developed <strong>hierarchy and control</strong>, where questioning a leader was treated as rebellion and keeping quiet about abuse was treated as protecting the gospel. These patterns help explain why victims were so often told to stay, submit and forgive, while abusers were protected. Ezekiel’s warning to the shepherds of Israel, who fed themselves and did not strengthen the weak or bind up the injured, but ruled them “with force and with rigor” (Ezek 34:4), has sadly found new fulfilments in every century. Shame says, “you are bad”, whereas healthy guilt says, “you did something wrong”. Shame-based communities often control people by threatening their belonging.",
      "We should be honest: none of this is unique to evangelicals. Self-erasing piety appears in Catholic, Orthodox, liberal Protestant and many non-Christian settings, and controlling communities exist in every tradition. And evangelicals also produced strong corrections. Cloud and Townsend’s <em>Boundaries</em> (Zondervan, 1992) taught a generation of believers that limits are biblical and that saying no can be an act of love. The problem is not one tribe. The problem is a picture of love that is not shaped by Christ. That is why the relational gospel keeps returning to Jesus. Wherever our inherited picture of love does not look like him, it is the picture that must change, not him. It is also fair to say that many of the people most hurt by these patterns have been helped back to health by other Christians: pastors, counsellors, friends and writers from every tradition who pointed them again to the Jesus of the Gospels."
    ],
    refs: ["Ps 22:6", "Job 25:6", "Rom 3:23", "Matt 23:4", "3 John 9-10", "Ezek 34:2-4"]
  },
  {
    id: "holy-love-is-whole",
    kicker: "Step 3 · The heart of God",
    title: "God’s love is holy, and holy means whole",
    body: [
      "Scripture says “God is love” (1 John 4:8, 16). It also says God is holy (Isa 6:3; 1 Pet 1:16). These are not two forces fighting inside God. God’s love is holy love: pure, undivided, healthy, and true. The English word “holy” is related to old words for “whole” and “healthy”, which is a helpful picture even though the Hebrew word <em>qadosh</em> itself carries the sense of being set apart. God’s love is set apart from all the counterfeits: it is not manipulative, not needy, not codependent, and not afraid. Holiness and love meet in the cross, where God’s pure goodness and his self-giving love are shown together (Rom 5:8).",
      "Because God’s love is whole, it wills the whole good of the one loved. That means God’s love can comfort and also confront. It can embrace and also say “stop”. Hebrews 12:6 says the Lord disciplines those he loves, and the point is training for life, not punishment for its own sake. A love that never says no is not more loving than God; it is less whole. In Jesus we meet a love that is perfectly secure, so it does not need to control anyone and does not need anyone’s approval to stay steady (John 13:3).",
      "This matters for us because we are made to love as God loves (Eph 5:1-2). If God’s love includes truth, limits and protection, then ours may too. A parent who never says no is not a better parent. A friend who never tells the truth is not a better friend. A church that never protects the vulnerable is not a more loving church. The same is true of Christian community. Paul tells the church to remove a man who was flagrantly harming others, so that he might be saved and the church protected (1 Cor 5:5). Holy love includes protection.",
      "In Christ we see this holy love perfectly. He welcomed sinners and ate with them (Luke 15:1-2). He also cleared the temple (John 2:13-17), rebuked Peter (Mark 8:33), and warned leaders who loaded heavy burdens on others (Matt 23:4). His love was never weak, and his strength was never unloving. So when we ask what love looks like, we do not start with what makes conflict go away, or with what keeps everyone pleased. We start with the Son who is “the radiance of his glory, the very image of his substance” (Heb 1:3). If Jesus could love perfectly and still say hard things, set limits and protect the vulnerable, then those things are not the opposite of love. They are part of its shape. The relational gospel calls this a love that is both tender and true, the kind of love the Father has for the Son and pours into our hearts by the Spirit (Rom 5:5).", "Holy love also has a goal: wholeness. The Hebrew idea of <em>shalom</em> is not just the absence of conflict but the presence of health, right relationships and flourishing. When love tolerates harm, it works against shalom. When love tells the truth, protects the vulnerable and invites repentance, it works toward it. That is why Jesus could say he came not to bring a false peace but a sword (Matt 10:34): his love sometimes disturbs unhealthy arrangements in order to heal them."
    ],
    refs: ["1 John 4:8", "1 John 4:16", "Isa 6:3", "Heb 12:6-11", "Eph 5:1-2", "Luke 15:1-2", "John 2:13-17", "Mark 8:33"]
  },
  {
    id: "god-respects-will",
    kicker: "Step 4 · The heart of God",
    title: "God does not coerce, and God says no",
    body: [
      "One of the most moving verses in the Gospels shows Jesus weeping over his city: “Jerusalem, Jerusalem... How often I would have gathered your children together, even as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings, and you would not!” (Matt 23:37). Jesus longs, invites and grieves. He does not force. Love that is real respects the will of the other. This is love that grieves a refusal rather than punishing it.",
      "This is a key thread of the relational gospel. God’s love is persuasive, not coercive. Theologians debate exactly how divine sovereignty and human freedom fit together, and Reformed and Arminian Christians answer differently. But both agree that God does not manipulate, bully or emotionally blackmail people. Love that controls is not the love revealed in Christ. Love that controls might win compliance, but it can never win a heart. That is why God’s way is invitation: “Behold, I stand at the door and knock” (Rev 3:20).",
      "God also says no. He said no to Moses entering the land (Deut 3:26). Jesus said no to the crowds who wanted to keep him in one town: “Let’s go elsewhere into the next towns” (Mark 1:38). He said no to being made king by force (John 6:15). He said no to judging a family inheritance dispute (Luke 12:14). He said no to his own brothers’ plan for his timing (John 7:6-8). He even “didn’t trust himself to them, because he knew everyone” (John 2:24). Each of these was a no that served a bigger yes: to his mission, to his Father’s timing, to the real good of the people around him.",
      "If God respects your will, then you may respect the will of others, and you may also expect others to respect yours. If God says no out of love, then your no can be love too. A no that protects, that tells the truth, or that refuses to join in harm is not a failure of love. It may be its clearest expression. This also changes how we hear God’s own limits. When God says no, it is not because he is stingy or controlling, but because he is love and wants our life (Deut 30:19). Learning to see God’s no as love can help us offer our own no without guilt, and receive other people’s no without resentment. A relationship where both people can say no safely is a relationship where yes really means something.", "Finally, notice that Jesus’ no was never cold. When he withdrew from the crowds, he went to pray. When he refused to be made king, he went on loving the same people. When he said no to his brothers, he still went to the feast in his own time (John 7:10). A godly no is not a rejection of the person; it is a refusal of a demand. You can turn down a request and still turn toward the person in love."
    ],
    refs: ["Matt 23:37", "Luke 13:34", "Mark 1:35-38", "John 6:15", "Luke 12:13-14", "John 7:6-8", "John 2:24-25", "Rev 3:20"]
  },
  {
    id: "trinity-union-without-merging",
    kicker: "Step 5 · The heart of God",
    title: "The Trinity: union without merging",
    body: [
      "The Christian God is one God in three persons: Father, Son and Holy Spirit. The early church fathers insisted on two things at once. The persons are truly <em>one</em>, sharing one life and love. And they are truly <em>distinct</em>, never blurred into each other. The Son is not the Father. The Spirit is not the Son. Yet they dwell in each other in perfect love (John 14:10-11; 17:21). Christians have confessed this in the Nicene Creed since the fourth century: the Son is “of one being with the Father”, and yet he is truly the Son, not the Father.",
      "Theologians called this mutual indwelling <em>perichoresis</em>. Writers like C. Baxter Kruger use the image of a “great dance” for it. That picture is beautiful, but it is an image: the Greek word does not literally mean “dance” (that idea comes from a similar-sounding verb, <em>perichoreuō</em>). What the word does describe is real: persons who make room for each other without losing themselves. The Cappadocian fathers, especially Gregory of Nazianzus, worked hard to say that the persons are one in being and yet each fully personal, never mere masks or modes of one person.",
      "This is the pattern of healthy love. In God, closeness does not swallow anyone up. Union does not mean merging. The Father does not erase the Son to love him; he delights in him (Matt 3:17). The Son does not dissolve into the Father to obey him; he freely and personally gives himself (John 10:18). Love in God has both intimacy and distinction. The Son’s obedience in the Gospels is never the obedience of someone erased or afraid. It is the joyful agreement of a Son who knows he is loved (John 5:19-20).",
      "So when someone says that a truly loving person has no self, no limits and no voice, the Trinity says otherwise. Healthy love includes a “you” and an “I” and a real “we”. Codependency merges; control dominates; isolation withdraws. Trinitarian love is different from all three: it is union with distinction, closeness with respect, giving without being erased. This picture can help us test our relationships. Do I have to disappear to be loved here? Do I make others disappear so I can feel safe? Or is there room for both of us?",
      "Jesus prays that we would share in this kind of union: “that they may be one, even as we are one” (John 17:22). This is the relational gospel’s great hope. We are drawn into the life of God, not to be absorbed and lost, but to become most truly ourselves in love. Maximus the Confessor and other Eastern fathers taught that union with God does not destroy what is human but perfects it. The same is true in our human relationships. A friendship, marriage or church that asks you to vanish is not reflecting the Trinity; one that makes room for you to be you, in love, is. That is why the relational gospel speaks of belonging before behaving. We do not earn a place at the table by disappearing. We are given a place, and in that place we learn to love."
    ],
    refs: ["John 14:10-11", "John 17:21-23", "Matt 3:16-17", "John 10:18", "John 16:13-15", "2 Cor 13:14"]
  },
  {
    id: "beloved-and-of-worth",
    kicker: "Step 6 · Who you are",
    title: "You are beloved and of real worth",
    body: [
      "Scripture’s first word about human beings is not “sinner” but “image”. “God created man in his own image. In God’s image he created him; male and female he created them” (Gen 1:27). Every person carries the dignity of the one whose image they bear. That is why harming a person is serious (Gen 9:6; James 3:9). The relational gospel reads this image-bearing not as a job description we must earn, but as a gift and identity that sin can mar but never erase.",
      "The psalmist prays, “I will give thanks to you, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made” (Ps 139:14). This is not pride. It is gratitude for God’s workmanship. Psalm 8 says God made human beings “a little lower than God” (or “the angels”) and crowned them “with glory and honor” (Ps 8:5). The same psalm says God knew us in our mother’s womb and saw our unformed substance (Ps 139:13-16). You were wanted and known before you did anything at all.",
      "In the New Testament the value placed on each person is shown at the cross. “You were bought with a price” (1 Cor 6:20). Your worth is not something you earn or lose; it is declared by what God was willing to give for you. If the Son of God gave himself for you (Gal 2:20), you cannot be worthless. Treating yourself as garbage is not humility; it disagrees with God’s own valuation. Jesus said that not one sparrow is forgotten before God, and “you are of more value than many sparrows” (Luke 12:6-7).",
      "The relational gospel adds that in Christ, God has joined himself to humanity. In the Incarnation the Son took our nature and made it his own. Early writers like Athanasius said he became what we are so that we might share what he is. Whatever else that means, it means human nature is not trash. It has been honoured by God himself. Irenaeus spoke of Christ recapitulating, that is, summing up and healing, the whole of human life in himself. The human story is not abandoned; it is taken up and restored.",
      "Traditional evangelical teaching rightly stresses that we are also fallen and that sin is real and serious (Rom 3:23). The relational gospel agrees, but says sin is a sickness in a beloved image-bearer, not the definition of who we are. You can be honest about your failings exactly because your worth is not on trial. That is the paradox of grace. People who know they are deeply loved are usually more able to admit wrong, not less, because confession no longer threatens their existence. And people who know their worth are usually less willing to tolerate abuse, not because they are proud, but because they have begun to agree with God about who they are (1 John 3:1). The relational gospel would add that Jesus did not die to make us worth something; he died because we already were, to him. If you have spent years believing you are worthless, receiving this may take time. Try reading Psalm 139 slowly, as if God is saying it to you, because he is."
    ],
    refs: ["Gen 1:26-27", "Gen 9:6", "Ps 8:4-5", "Ps 139:13-14", "1 Cor 6:19-20", "Gal 2:20", "James 3:9", "Zeph 3:17"]
  },
  {
    id: "love-as-yourself",
    kicker: "Step 7 · The command",
    title: "Love your neighbour as yourself: not only, but also",
    body: [
      "Jesus summed up the Law in two commands: love God with everything, and “You shall love your neighbor as yourself” (Matt 22:39, quoting Lev 19:18). The word “as” matters. The command assumes that a healthy person already cares for their own life, and asks us to extend that same care to others. It does not say “instead of yourself”. In the original setting of Leviticus 19, the command sits among very practical instructions: paying workers on time, not cursing the deaf or tripping the blind, judging fairly, not slandering (Lev 19:13-16). Love of neighbour is concrete, and so is the care for one’s own life that it takes for granted. Jesus’ parable of the Good Samaritan widens “neighbour” to include the stranger and even the enemy (Luke 10:29-37), but it does not abolish the “as yourself”. The second command is “like” the first (22:39): love of God and love of people belong together, and love of people includes the person you are.",
      "Paul makes the same assumption about marriage: “husbands also ought to love their own wives as their own bodies... For no man ever hated his own flesh; but nourishes and cherishes it, even as the Lord also does the assembly” (Eph 5:28-29). Nourishing and cherishing yourself is treated as normal, and even compared to how Christ cares for his church. Paul repeats the same assumption in his letters to the Romans and Galatians: the whole Law is summed up in loving your neighbour as yourself (Rom 13:9; Gal 5:14).",
      "Philippians 2:3-4 is often quoted as if it forbids any concern for yourself. But read it carefully: “in humility, each counting others better than himself; each of you not just looking to his own things, but each of you also to the things of others.” The Greek has <em>not only... but also</em> (<em>mē... monon... alla kai</em> in many manuscripts). Paul assumes you will look to your own interests, and asks you not to look <em>only</em> there. Some translations soften or drop the word “only”, and a few manuscripts lack it, but the “but also” (<em>alla kai</em>) is secure, and the sense is the same: concern for others is added to proper concern for oneself, not substituted for it. Paul then describes Christ, who did not grasp at equality with God but emptied himself (2:5-8). Christ’s self-giving is the model of love, and it was the free act of someone completely secure in who he was.",
      "So Scripture holds together two things. Self-centredness, where only my interests count, is condemned (Phil 2:21; 2 Tim 3:2). But care for one’s own life is assumed and even commanded (Acts 20:28, “Take heed to yourselves”; 1 Tim 4:16). Augustine said there was no need for a command that we love ourselves, because we already do; the danger is loving ourselves wrongly. Aquinas argued that, out of charity, a person should love himself because he belongs to God. Jesus himself did this. He fed thousands, and he also went away to eat and rest with his friends (Mark 6:31-32). He gave his life for the world, and he also received the anointing of a woman who loved him, defending her gift against those who called it waste (Mark 14:3-9).",
      "Some fear this opens the door to selfishness. That is a fair caution. The answer is not to stop caring for yourself, but to let your self-care be shaped by love: rest so you can love well, keep healthy limits so you can give freely, and honour your own life because God honours it. Love of self and love of neighbour rise and fall together. People who despise themselves often struggle to love others well: they may become needy, controlling, or quietly resentful. People who know they are loved can give freely, because their giving is not a desperate attempt to earn a place. The relational gospel locates that security in the Father’s love revealed in Jesus: you are already included, already beloved, so you can love without grasping. In practice this might look like keeping a regular day of rest, saying no to a commitment that would leave your family neglected, or seeing a doctor instead of pushing through. None of these are selfish. They are ways of caring for the life God gave you so that you can keep loving."
    ],
    refs: ["Matt 22:37-40", "Lev 19:18", "Eph 5:28-29", "Phil 2:3-4", "Phil 2:21", "Acts 20:28", "1 Tim 4:16", "Rom 13:9"]
  },
  {
    id: "burdens-and-loads",
    kicker: "Step 8 · Shared and personal",
    title: "Carry one another’s burdens, and carry your own load",
    body: [
      "Galatians 6 seems at first to contradict itself. Verse 2: “Bear one another’s burdens, and so fulfill the law of Christ.” Verse 5: “For each man will bear his own burden.” Which is it? The Greek uses two different words, and the difference is very helpful. Many readers have been confused by these two verses, and some were taught to always carry everything for everyone. The Greek clears this up. Both statements are true at once, and holding them together is one of the most practical gifts in the whole New Testament for anyone who struggles with guilt about saying no.",
      "In verse 2 the word is <em>baros</em>, a heavy weight. Thayer’s lexicon uses the word “heaviness” and lists “weight, burden, trouble”. These are the crushing loads that no one can carry alone: grief, crisis, illness, temptation, overwhelming need. Love runs toward these. The context (6:1) is restoring someone caught in a fault, gently. Thayer’s lexicon even suggests that in this verse the burdens in view are “troublesome moral faults”, so that the sense is close to “bear with one another’s faults”. Either way, verse 2 is about the heavy things, the moments when someone is overwhelmed, and the community of Christ steps in to help carry them.",
      "In verse 5 the word is <em>phortion</em>, a load or pack. Bible Hub’s HELPS word-study describes it as “a burden which must be carried by the individual”. This is the everyday responsibility each person carries: their own choices, attitudes, work and growth. Nobody else can carry it for you, and you cannot carry it for someone else. (Lexicons note the word can be used in other senses elsewhere, so the distinction is about this passage, not a rule for every use.) Paul’s point in verse 5 is also that each person will answer for their own work (6:4). You cannot take responsibility for another adult’s choices, and you are not meant to.",
      "This gives a simple test. Is someone being crushed by a <em>baros</em>, something too heavy for one person? Then love helps. Or is someone asking you to carry their <em>phortion</em>, the ordinary responsibility that belongs to them? Then love may say no, because carrying it would keep them from growing and wear you out. Paul can say both “help” and “carry your own” in the same breath. So love helps carry a friend’s overwhelming grief, but it does not do their homework, pay their gambling debts forever, or manage their emotions for them.",
      "Cloud and Townsend made this distinction famous in <em>Boundaries</em>, and it is one of the clearest evangelical contributions to the topic. It frees us from two errors: the coldness that never helps, and the rescuing that never lets others grow up. It also helps us read Jesus. He carried the crushing weight no one else could carry, our sin, sickness and death (Isa 53:4; Matt 8:17). But he did not do for people what they needed to do themselves. He told the healed man to pick up his own mat (John 5:8). He asked the blind man, “What do you want me to do for you?” (Mark 10:51). His help always honoured the person’s own agency and dignity. Many people find it helpful to ask the question out loud, kindly: “Is this something you can’t carry, or something you’d rather not carry?” The honest answer often clarifies what love requires."
    ],
    refs: ["Gal 6:1-5", "Gal 6:2", "Gal 6:5", "2 Thess 3:10-12", "Rom 14:12", "Prov 19:19"]
  },
  {
    id: "love-rejoices-in-truth",
    kicker: "Step 9 · Love and truth",
    title: "Love rejoices in the truth and does not enable evil",
    body: [
      "Paul’s great love chapter says that love “doesn’t rejoice in unrighteousness, but rejoices with the truth” (1 Cor 13:6). Love is not neutral about harm. It does not cover up abuse, smile at lies, or help someone keep destroying themselves or others. Love is glad when the truth comes to light. This is not the same as being critical or suspicious. It is a love that wants what is real and good for everyone involved. Paul places this right in the middle of his description of love, alongside patience and kindness. Truthfulness is not the enemy of love; it is one of love’s own features.",
      "Ephesians 4:15 calls us to speak “truth in love”. Both words matter. Truth without love can wound for the sake of winning. Love without truth can flatter for the sake of comfort. Proverbs says it bluntly: “Faithful are the wounds of a friend; but the kisses of an enemy are deceitful” (Prov 27:6, KJV). A friend who tells you a hard truth may love you more than one who only tells you what you want to hear. Jesus spoke truth to people he loved: to Peter, to Martha, to the rich young man whom he “loved” and then told the hard truth (Mark 10:21).",
      "The Law of Moses even links honesty and love: “You shall not hate your brother in your heart. You shall surely rebuke your neighbor, and not bear sin because of him... you shall love your neighbor as yourself” (Lev 19:17-18). Silence about wrong can be a form of hatred, because it leaves the other person in their harm. That is a striking idea for those of us raised to think that keeping quiet is always the kind thing. Leviticus pairs honest rebuke with the command not to take revenge or bear a grudge. Truth-telling is the alternative to resentment, not its expression.",
      "“Enabling” is a modern word, but the idea is old. Proverbs warns that if you rescue a hot-tempered person you will have to do it again (Prov 19:19). Paul tells the Thessalonians not to keep feeding those who refuse to work (2 Thess 3:10). Love sometimes means letting a person face the natural consequences of their choices, because those consequences may be the very thing that wakes them up (Luke 15:14-17). The father of the prodigal son did not chase him to the far country with more money. He let him go, waited, and ran to meet him when he came home (Luke 15:20).",
      "This does not license harshness. Paul says we should restore others “in a spirit of gentleness” and watch ourselves (Gal 6:1). The aim of truth-telling is always healing and restoration, never humiliation. A simple way to check your heart before confronting is to ask: Am I hoping this person will be healed, or hoping they will be hurt? Am I willing to hear their side? Am I also open to my own part? When the answer is love, truth can be spoken with both courage and tenderness. In the end, love that tells the truth is a form of respect. It treats the other person as an adult who can face reality and change, not as someone too fragile to hear the truth."
    ],
    refs: ["1 Cor 13:6", "Eph 4:15", "Eph 4:25", "Prov 27:6", "Lev 19:17-18", "Prov 19:19", "2 Thess 3:10", "Luke 15:14-17", "Gal 6:1"]
  },
  {
    id: "anger-and-meekness",
    kicker: "Step 10 · Strength under love",
    title: "Healthy anger, and meekness that is strong",
    body: [
      "Many Christians learned that all anger is sin. But Scripture says, “Be angry, and don’t sin. Don’t let the sun go down on your wrath” (Eph 4:26, quoting Ps 4:4). Anger itself is not the sin. It is a signal that something we value is threatened or violated. The question is what we do with it. The psalms are full of honest anger brought to God: anger at injustice, at betrayal, at the suffering of the poor. God does not reject these prayers. He includes them in Scripture. Anger brought into the light of God’s love can become the energy for courage, protection and justice, rather than poison in the heart. Many of us learned to swallow anger until it came out sideways as resentment, sarcasm or illness. Scripture offers a better way: name it, bring it to God, and let it lead to wise action.",
      "Jesus was angry. “When he had looked around at them with anger, being grieved at the hardening of their hearts, he said to the man, ‘Stretch out your hand’” (Mark 3:5). His anger was joined with grief and led to healing. He was also moved to strong action when the temple was turned into a market (John 2:15-17). Healthy anger protects, names wrong, and moves toward repair; unhealthy anger attacks, broods and seeks revenge (Eph 4:31; James 1:20). The difference is not whether we feel anger, but whether anger serves love or replaces it.",
      "Then there is meekness. Jesus says, “Blessed are the gentle” (Matt 5:5, WEB; KJV “meek”) and calls himself “gentle and lowly in heart” (Matt 11:29). The Greek word is <em>praus</em>. Bible Hub’s summary describes it as “inward gentleness that manifests as humble strength under God’s control”. In Greek ethics, Aristotle used the related noun <em>praotēs</em> for the healthy middle point concerning anger: neither explosive nor spineless. Bible Hub also lists the meanings “meek, gentle, humble”. The word describes a person who is not harsh or easily provoked, and who is teachable before God.",
      "You may have heard that <em>praus</em> was used of a “trained war horse”, powerful but under control. That is a popular sermon illustration, not an established lexical meaning, so hold it lightly. But the general point is sound. Meekness in the Bible is not weakness. Moses was called very meek (Num 12:3), yet he confronted Pharaoh. Jesus was gentle, yet he faced down religious leaders and walked straight into Jerusalem. Galatians 5:23 lists gentleness as fruit of the Spirit; it grows from the inside out as we rest in God’s love, not by pressing down our feelings.",
      "So gentleness is not the absence of strength. It is strength that will not crush, and anger that will not destroy. You can be gentle and still say no. You can be meek and still stand up for someone being harmed. For many people, especially those who grew up being told their anger was sinful, learning to notice anger is the first step to noticing that they are being harmed. Anger can then be brought to God honestly, as the psalmists did (Ps 109; Ps 137), and turned into wise, non-revengeful action. The Christian tradition has sometimes called this the “ordering” of the passions: not killing anger or desire, but bringing them under love, so that they serve life."
    ],
    refs: ["Eph 4:26", "Ps 4:4", "Mark 3:5", "John 2:15-17", "Eph 4:31", "James 1:19-20", "Matt 5:5", "Matt 11:29", "Num 12:3", "Gal 5:23"]
  },
  {
    id: "cheek-and-mile",
    kicker: "Step 11 · Hard sayings",
    title: "Turning the other cheek and going the second mile",
    body: [
      "Jesus said, “Don’t resist him who is evil; but whoever strikes you on your right cheek, turn to him the other also... Whoever compels you to go one mile, go with him two” (Matt 5:39-41). These words have often been used to tell victims to stay and take more. Read in context, they say something very different. The same chapter also tells us to love our enemies and pray for those who persecute us (5:44), which shows that the aim is not passivity but a different kind of power. Context matters for every hard saying, and Jesus’ own example is the best commentary on his words.",
      "Jesus is speaking about personal insult and revenge. The context is “An eye for an eye” (5:38), the law of limited retaliation. Jesus forbids hitting back, not seeking safety. A slap on the right cheek (with the right hand) would most naturally be a backhanded blow, an insult in that culture. Walter Wink famously argued that turning the other cheek, giving the cloak, and going the second mile were creative, nonviolent ways to refuse humiliation and expose injustice. Many scholars find this helpful; others think it reads too much into the text. It is an interpretation, not settled fact.",
      "Whatever we think of Wink, Jesus’ own life shows that “turn the other cheek” does not mean “let anyone harm you”. When an officer struck him, Jesus did not stay silent: “If I have spoken evil, testify of the evil; but if well, why do you beat me?” (John 18:23). He slipped away from a mob trying to throw him off a cliff (Luke 4:29-30). He hid from those who wanted to stone him (John 8:59). He avoided Judea when people were seeking to kill him (John 7:1). John 18:23 is especially important because it is Jesus’ own commentary, in action, on what turning the cheek does and does not mean.",
      "The second mile refers to the Roman practice of compelling civilians to carry a soldier’s pack (as Simon of Cyrene was compelled to carry the cross, Mark 15:21). Going further than required could turn forced labour into a free gift and quietly reclaim one’s dignity. It is about the freedom of love, not about endless exploitation. The common thread in all three examples (the cheek, the cloak, the mile) is that the person treated as an object becomes a free agent again. They choose their response instead of being controlled by the other person’s violence. Whatever historical reading one prefers, the saying is about refusing to let evil dictate one’s heart, not about surrendering to it.",
      "So these sayings teach us not to retaliate, not to return insult for insult (1 Pet 3:9; Rom 12:17-21). They do not cancel safety, justice, or the right to leave. A woman being beaten is not being asked by Jesus to stay. Jesus forbids revenge; he does not command remaining in harm. The relational gospel sees in these sayings the nonviolent heart of God revealed in Christ, who overcame evil not by copying it but by absorbing and forgiving it. That is why Christians are called to break cycles of retaliation. But absorbing an insult in freedom is very different from being trapped in a pattern of abuse that God himself hates (Ps 11:5). If you have been told that leaving harm is disobedience to this verse, it may help to read it slowly alongside the story of Jesus’ own life. You will find that he refused revenge, and he also refused to be anyone’s victim before his hour."
    ],
    refs: ["Matt 5:38-42", "Luke 6:29", "John 18:22-23", "Luke 4:28-30", "John 8:59", "John 7:1", "Mark 15:21", "Rom 12:17-21", "1 Pet 3:9"]
  },
  {
    id: "forgive-reconcile-trust",
    kicker: "Step 12 · Three different things",
    title: "Forgiveness, reconciliation and trust are not the same",
    body: [
      "Many people were taught that forgiving means forgetting, reconciling, and trusting again, all at once. But Scripture treats these as three different things. Mixing them up has kept many people in danger. Survivors have been told to hug their abuser at church, to drop police reports, or to let a dangerous relative back into the home, all in the name of forgiveness. Untangling the three ideas can bring enormous relief.",
      "<strong>Forgiveness</strong> is releasing a debt. The common Greek word <em>aphiēmi</em> means to let go or send away. It is a gift you give, mostly in your own heart before God: you give up the right to revenge and hand the case to God (Rom 12:19). Jesus calls us to forgive freely and repeatedly (Matt 18:21-22; Col 3:13). You can forgive someone who never apologises, someone who is dead, or someone you must never see again. Christians debate whether full forgiveness in Luke 17:3-4 depends on repentance; many distinguish an inner letting go that is always possible from a spoken, completed forgiveness that responds to repentance. Forgiveness also does not mean saying that what happened was acceptable. Jesus never asks you to call evil good (Isa 5:20). Forgiveness takes the wrong so seriously that only God can carry the debt.",
      "<strong>Reconciliation</strong> is restoring a relationship, and it takes two. Paul says, “If it is possible, as much as it is up to you, be at peace with all men” (Rom 12:18). Notice: “if it is possible” and “as much as it is up to you”. Sometimes it is not possible, because the other person has not changed or is not safe. Reconciliation in Scripture is never cheap. Zacchaeus’ repentance led him to repay those he had cheated fourfold (Luke 19:8).",
      "<strong>Trust</strong> is confidence that someone will act well in the future. It is rebuilt over time through changed behaviour. Jesus forgave freely, but “didn’t trust himself to them” (John 2:24). Joseph forgave his brothers, but first tested whether they had changed (Gen 42–45). Forgiveness can be immediate; trust may take years, or may never be wise. This matters especially in abuse, where an abuser may apologise quickly and demand to be trusted again at once. Real repentance accepts consequences, makes restitution where possible (Luke 19:8), and does not insist on its rights. If someone uses “you have to forgive me” to regain control, that is not repentance; it is manipulation in spiritual language.",
      "And forgetting? God says he will “remember their sins no more” (Jer 31:34), which means he will not hold them against us, not that he has amnesia. For us, remembering harm can be wise and protective. You can forgive and still remember, still set limits, still report a crime, still keep children away from someone dangerous. That is not unforgiveness. That is love and wisdom together. Many survivors have been told they are “bitter” or “unforgiving” because they will not return to someone who hurt them. That is a misuse of the gospel. You can forgive from a distance, and you can let God be the judge while also letting the proper authorities do their work (Rom 13:4). Some survivors find it helpful to say, “I forgive you, and I am also keeping my distance.” Those two sentences can sit together with integrity."
    ],
    refs: ["Matt 18:21-22", "Col 3:13", "Rom 12:18-19", "Luke 17:3-4", "John 2:24", "Gen 42:15-16", "Gen 45:4-15", "Jer 31:34", "Matt 6:12"]
  },
  {
    id: "open-hearted-dignified",
    kicker: "Step 13 · Soft but not naive",
    title: "Open-hearted, not naive: honour for yourself and others",
    body: [
      "Are Christians meant to be soft and easy to attack? Jesus sent his disciples out “as sheep among wolves. Therefore be wise as serpents, and harmless as doves” (Matt 10:16). Notice both halves. Harmless, yes: not scheming, not vengeful. But also wise: aware of danger, not naive about wolves. Proverbs adds, “A prudent man sees danger, and hides himself” (Prov 22:3). Jesus was not naive. He knew what was in people (John 2:25), and he warned his disciples to beware of false prophets who come in sheep’s clothing (Matt 7:15). Doves are gentle; serpents are alert. Jesus asks for both at once, which means Christians are not meant to be gullible.",
      "“Keep your heart with all diligence, for out of it is the wellspring of life” (Prov 4:23). Guarding your heart is not closing it. It is caring for it like a spring that must stay clean. We are called to be open-hearted (2 Cor 6:11-13), but vulnerability is wisest with safe people: those who have shown they will handle your heart with care. Even Jesus shared his deepest anguish in Gethsemane with three close friends, not with the crowd (Matt 26:37-38). Being open-hearted does not mean telling everyone everything; it means not shutting yourself away from love. Wisdom means discerning who has earned the right to hear your heart.",
      "Honour runs in every direction. “In honor preferring one another” (Rom 12:10). “Honor all men” (1 Pet 2:17). All means all, and that includes you. You cannot truly honour every person while treating yourself as the one exception. Honour means treating someone as weighty, as worth taking seriously; the Hebrew word for glory and honour, <em>kavod</em>, comes from a root meaning heavy. To honour yourself is to take your own life, body, feelings and calling seriously before God, not to inflate yourself above others.",
      "Paul himself insisted on his dignity when it mattered. After being beaten and jailed without trial in Philippi, he refused to be quietly released: “They have beaten us publicly, without a trial, men who are Romans... let them come themselves and bring us out!” (Acts 16:37). Later, about to be flogged, he asked, “Is it lawful for you to scourge a man who is a Roman, and not found guilty?” (Acts 22:25). He appealed to Caesar when he could not get justice (Acts 25:11). Using legitimate rights is not a lack of faith. Paul did this not out of pride but out of concern for justice and for the young church in Philippi, which would be safer if the authorities had to admit they had acted unlawfully. Standing up for your dignity can protect others too.",
      "This is the shape of love the relational gospel sees in Christ: open, warm, generous and forgiving, and also wise, truthful, protected and dignified. It is the love of someone who knows they are beloved, and so does not need to grasp, and does not need to be trampled. This is love with a spine."
    ],
    refs: ["Matt 10:16", "Prov 22:3", "Prov 4:23", "2 Cor 6:11-13", "Matt 26:37-38", "Rom 12:10", "1 Pet 2:17", "Acts 16:37", "Acts 22:25", "Acts 25:11"]
  }
];

