    q: "Isn’t self-love a modern idea that the church never taught?",
    a: "Not at all. Augustine wrote in <em>On Christian Doctrine</em> that there is no need for a command to love ourselves, because we naturally do; the question is loving ourselves rightly, in God, and not for our own sake alone. Bernard of Clairvaux’s <em>On Loving God</em> describes four degrees of love whose highest stage is loving oneself “only in God”, that is, for God’s sake. On the way there, he says that our natural self-love grows “truly social” when it includes our neighbours. Thomas Aquinas argued that a person should love himself out of charity, because he belongs to God, and he distinguished this from the disordered self-love of the wicked. Kierkegaard, in <em>Works of Love</em>, said that loving yourself rightly and loving your neighbour belong together. These writers also warned strongly against selfish self-love, and none of them would recognise a self-love that ignores God and others. The tradition does not ask us to hate ourselves, but to love ourselves rightly. What is modern is not self-love but, in some places, the loss of the word “rightly”. So the question for Christians is not whether to love themselves, but how. Self-love that is grateful, humble, honest and generous is very old Christian wisdom. Self-love that is grasping, vain and closed to others is the old self that Christ came to heal. That one word, “rightly”, is the difference between the self-love Scripture warns against and the self-love it assumes."
